## The Illusion of Fixed Frontiers

Prior to the institutional cartography established by the Survey of India, riparian delta boundaries were fundamentally dynamic. Rivers like the Padma, Meghna, and Jamuna shifted courses with seasonal monsoons, rendering cadastral borders temporary and negotiable.

## Rennell's Bengal Atlas (1779)

Major James Rennell's *Bengal Atlas* represented the earliest systematic triangulation of the lower Gangetic plain. Rather than recording communal divisions, these surveys mapped revenue tracts, navigation routes, and fortress strongholds.

> "To map a delta is to map time in motion; the silt deposited by one flood creates the disputed district of the next generation."

### The Radcliffe Boundary Arbitrations

In August 1947, Sir Cyril Radcliffe's boundary commissions were tasked with drawing international borders across contiguous districts within forty days. The resulting line severed contiguous rail systems, waterways, and ancestral homesteads, establishing structural anomalies that persisted for decades.
